<p>The North Dakota Memorial Bridge, later renamed the North Dakota Liberty Memorial Bridge, was the first automobile bridge across the Missouri River in North Dakota. It carried State Highway 6 beginning in 1922 and U.S. Highway 10 beginning in 1926. The bridge honored the men and women who served and died in World War I. In 1924, the North Dakota chapter of American War Mothers installed prairie boulders bearing memorial plaques at each end.</p>

<p>The structure used Warren-Turner trusses that spanned 200 feet above the bottom of the lowest pier. Workers drove 8,000 rivets into the trusses.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/gone-forever/north-dakota-memorial-bridge/210-carey-family-lm-bridge.jpg" alt="David Carey and family associated with the Memorial Bridge">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>David Carey, his wife, and their four children arrived in 1879. John McGinley rowed them across the Missouri River in a skiff during a severe windstorm, and they nearly died. Carey had moved from Illinois to work on the Northern Pacific Railway bridge and helped build North Dakota's first railroad bridge across the Missouri.</p>

<p>When the automobile bridge opened, the 86-year-old Carey waited for hours so he could be the first person to cross it by car. “I am thankful I lived to see this day,” he said.</p>

