<p>Concrete approaches lined with North Dakota-made brick extended 400 feet on the Bismarck side and 625 feet on the Mandan side. The bridge deck and approaches were 26 feet 6 inches wide, with pedestrian walkways 4 feet 4 inches wide. The eastern river pier extended about 41 feet below normal water level and 101 feet below the roadway. The western pier extended about 74 feet below the water and 134 feet below the deck.</p>

<p>Crews used the open-well method to construct the piers. Employment peaked at 250, with work continuing day and night. Ice and an unusually high June river rise each destroyed the temporary construction trestle once. Construction began in 1920, and a three-day dedication was held in September 1922. The {% inflation 1358000, 1920 %} cost was shared by the federal government, North Dakota, and Morton and Burleigh counties.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/gone-forever/north-dakota-memorial-bridge/240-1926-memrl-hwy-plaque.jpg" alt="Memorial Highway plaque installed near the bridge">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>The bridge initially connected Mandan and Bismarck as part of State Highway 6. The four-mile rural roadway ran from East Main Street in Mandan to Front Avenue and Hannifin Street in Bismarck. Bismarck's West Main Avenue reached that intersection through a three-block dogleg beneath a railroad viaduct. The route was paved with two lanes of concrete in 1924 and renamed Memorial Highway on May 30, 1925, in honor of Americans who died in military service.</p>

<p>Construction of a four-lane replacement began in 2006 after deicing salts corroded grout in the original stone piers. The new {% place "liberty-memorial-bridge" %}Liberty Memorial Bridge{% endplace %}, dedicated November 11, 2008, honors all veterans who died in service. Explosive charges demolished the original bridge, which was removed from the riverbed in sections. Several additional efforts were needed to remove enough submerged material for safe boat passage.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/gone-forever/north-dakota-memorial-bridge/188-m-hwy-st-sign-east-derated.jpg" alt="Memorial Highway street sign">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>A direct connection to the western end of Bismarck's Main Avenue opened in 1959. A viaduct about 200 feet southeast of the former grade crossing allowed Main and Rosser avenues to extend directly west beneath the railroad tracks.</p>

<p>Before 1959, most traffic east of the bridge followed Memorial Highway—West Front Avenue—and reached Main Avenue through the dogleg road and viaduct between Washington and Hannifin streets. The dogleg was eliminated during the 1994 Washington Street reconstruction, when new bridges were installed for Main Avenue and the railroad.</p>
